# Connect to your Redis server

Your Redis instance is running, password-protected (`requirepass`), and reachable
on the address below.

- **Host:** `{{ .Host }}`
- **Port:** `{{ .Port }}`

Redis `requirepass` has no username — you authenticate with the password alone
(the ACL `default` user).

## redis-cli

```bash
redis-cli -h {{ .Host }} -p {{ .Port }}
# then authenticate:
127.0.0.1:{{ .Port }}> AUTH <password>
```

Avoid putting the password on the command line (`-a`), where it is visible in the
process list; use the `REDISCLI_AUTH` environment variable instead:

```bash
REDISCLI_AUTH=<password> redis-cli -h {{ .Host }} -p {{ .Port }}
```

## Password

The `requirepass` value is **not shown here and is never stored by Proxcloud**. It
was displayed **once**, at creation time, in the provisioning response — save it in
your password manager now. If you did not capture it, connect over SSH and rotate
it in-guest:

```bash
redis-cli CONFIG SET requirepass 'a-new-strong-password'
REDISCLI_AUTH='a-new-strong-password' redis-cli CONFIG REWRITE
```

## Security note (READ THIS)

**Do NOT expose Redis to the public internet.** Even with `requirepass`, Redis is a
frequent target and a compromised instance can be used for remote code execution.
This server binds all interfaces so you can reach it from your LAN — keep it behind
the guest firewall and restrict access to trusted hosts on a private network, or
reach it only over an SSH tunnel. See the service's exposed ports ({{ .Port }}/tcp).
